---
// Spotting scams. MODERATION_AND_TRUST.md §2 — the anti-scam posture, made useful
// to a reader rather than stated as a policy.
//
// This page exists because the audience is the target. Opportunity scams work
// precisely on people who are looking hard for a way forward, and the single most
// valuable thing this product can do is make the pattern recognisable — including
// when the impersonator is pretending to be us.

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { BRAND, CONTACT, NO_FEE_STATEMENT } from "@mbele/config";

const SIGNS: [string, string][] = [
  [
    "A fee to apply, to register, or to be shortlisted",
    "This is the clearest signal there is. Real grants, scholarships and competitions do not charge you to be considered. A \"processing fee\", an \"administration charge\" or a \"refundable deposit\" all mean the same thing.",
  ],
  [
    "Money before money",
    "Any request to pay for a visa, a courier, a certificate, a training pack or a bank transfer fee in order to receive an award. Real organisers pay their own costs.",
  ],
  [
    "Your bank details, your card, or a crypto wallet",
    "No legitimate application needs your card number. Payment details come at the end of a real award process, through the organisation's own finance team, never over chat.",
  ],
  [
    "Urgency that does not match the deadline",
    "\"Reply in the next 2 hours or you lose your place.\" Real deadlines are published and do not move by the hour. Urgency is there to stop you checking.",
  ],
  [
    "A prize you did not apply for",
    "You cannot win something you never entered. This is the oldest version of the trick and it still works because it is flattering.",
  ],
  [
    "A free email address or a lookalike domain",
    "An organisation's programme officer writes from the organisation's own domain. Check the spelling character by character — one swapped letter is the whole scam.",
  ],
  [
    "Contact only through WhatsApp or Telegram, with no website",
    "A real programme has a page you can find independently. If every link goes back to the same chat, there is nothing behind it.",
  ],
  [
    "An interview that is only ever a chat",
    "No video, no call, no name you can look up, and a job offer within an hour.",
  ],
  [
    "Pressure to keep it quiet",
    "\"Do not discuss this with anyone until the announcement.\" Secrecy protects the scam, never you.",
  ],
];
---

<Base
  title="Spotting scams"
  description="How to tell a real opportunity from a scam, and what we do to keep them off this site."
  canonical="/anti-scam"
>
  <article class="max-w-prose">
    <h1 class="text-title font-semibold">Spotting scams</h1>

    <p class="mt-3 border-l-[3px] border-brand bg-brand-wash p-3 text-dense font-semibold">
      {NO_FEE_STATEMENT}
    </p>

    <p class="mt-4 text-dense">
      Opportunity scams work on people who are looking hard for a way forward, which is everyone
      reading this. The pattern is always the same once you have seen it, so here it is.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-subtitle font-semibold">Nine signs, in order of how reliable they are</h2>
    <ol class="mt-3 space-y-4">
      {
        SIGNS.map(([sign, why], i) => (
          <li>
            <p class="text-dense font-semibold">
              {i + 1}. {sign}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-dense text-ink-2">{why}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-subtitle font-semibold">The one rule that catches almost everything</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dense">
      Find the organisation independently. Do not click the link you were sent — search for the
      organisation's name, go to its own website, and look for the programme there. If it does not
      exist on the organiser's own site, it does not exist. This one habit defeats nearly every
      version of this scam.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-subtitle font-semibold">If someone claims to be us</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dense">
      We will never ask you for money, for your bank or card details, or for a password. We do not
      have a recruitment team, we do not offer paid coaching, and we do not run a paid fast-track
      for applications. Everything we send carries a reason it was sent and a way to stop it. Our
      bot never asks for payment.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dense">
      If you get something claiming to be from {BRAND.name} that does not match that, it is not us.
      Forward it to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.security}`} class="text-brand">{CONTACT.security}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-subtitle font-semibold">What we do on our side</h2>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-dense">
      <li>
        Nothing that charges a fee to apply is ever published here. That is not a guideline — the
        database refuses to store it.
      </li>
      <li>Every published opportunity carries a link to the organiser's own page and the date we last checked it.</li>
      <li>Links are checked against a malware and phishing list before an opportunity goes live, and again on a schedule.</li>
      <li>Anything reported is looked at by a person, and anything with a credible safety concern comes down while we look.</li>
      <li>Reporting takes one tap and needs no account.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-8 text-subtitle font-semibold">Found one here?</h2>
    <p class="mt-2 text-dense">
      Tell us. Use the report link on the opportunity page, or write to
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.support}`} class="text-brand">{CONTACT.support}</a>. You do not
      need to be sure, and you do not need to explain yourself well — "this feels wrong" is a
      perfectly good report, and we would much rather check ten honest listings than miss one scam.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
